<?phem

namespace App\Ui\Demos;

use Phem\Attribute\Page;
use Phem\Node;
use Phem\Ui\Base\Examples\{ToastCustomDemo, ToastDeduplicatedDemo, ToastHeroDemo, ToastPositionDemo, ToastPromiseDemo, ToastUndoDemo, ToastVaryingHeightsDemo};

use function App\Ui\{DemoPage, Example, Examples, Hero};
use function Phem\Ui\Base\Examples\ToastAnchoredDemo;

#[Page('/components/toast')]
function ToastPage(): Node
{
    return (
        <DemoPage slug="toast">
            <Hero source="ToastHeroDemo">
                <ToastHeroDemo />
            </Hero>
            <Examples>
                <Example title="Anchored toasts" source="ToastAnchoredDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>Toasts can be anchored to a specific element: give the toast the <code>anchor</code> option with the <code>id</code> of the element. This is useful for showing contextual feedback like transient "Copied" toasts that appear near the button that triggered the action.</p>
                        <p>Anchored toasts should be rendered in a separate <code>ToastViewport</code> from stacked toasts: an <code>anchored</code> viewport with a <code>name</code>, and the <code>viewport</code> option of the toast. The anchored viewport places each toast on its <code>side</code>, with its <code>sideOffset</code>.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <ToastAnchoredDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Custom position" source="ToastPositionDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>The position of the toasts is controlled by the <code>position</code> prop of <code>ToastViewport</code>: <code>bottom-right</code> (the default) or <code>top-center</code>. A toast at the top closes with a swipe up. The following shows a top-center position.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <ToastPositionDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Undo action" source="ToastUndoDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>When adding a toast, the <code>action</code> option gives the label of an action button inside of it. A click on the button closes the toast and calls the <code>onCall</code> handler of the viewport, with the <code>action</code>, the <code>id</code> and the <code>data</code> of the toast in <code>$e-&gt;detail</code>: this enables the ability to undo an action associated with the toast.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <ToastUndoDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Promise" source="ToastPromiseDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>An asynchronous toast can be created with three possible states: <code>loading</code>, <code>success</code>, and <code>error</code>. The <code>type</code> string matches these states to change the styling.</p>
                        <p>Here, the button has <code>data-phem-toast-show</code> with the detail of a <code>loading</code> toast: it shows at the click, before the server answers. The handler answers after two seconds with a <code>success</code> or an <code>error</code> toast with the same <code>id</code>, which updates the loading toast.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <ToastPromiseDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Custom" source="ToastCustomDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>A toast with custom data can be created by passing an array to the <code>data</code> option. Each <code>ToastData</code> part of the template of the viewport shows the value of its <code>name</code>.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <ToastCustomDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Deduplicated toast" source="ToastDeduplicatedDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>When you show the same toast by <code>id</code>, the toast on the screen updates, and its <code>data-update-key</code> attribute counts the updates. Its <code>data-updated</code> attribute comes again with each update, so a CSS animation on <code>[data-updated]</code> plays again while the same toast stays on the screen.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <ToastDeduplicatedDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Varying heights" source="ToastVaryingHeightsDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>Toasts with varying heights are stacked by clamping every toast's height to the frontmost toast at index 0 using the <code>--toast-frontmost-height</code> CSS variable, while the <code>data-behind</code> attribute hides the content of the toasts behind it. Avoid sizing <code>ToastContent</code> to the root's height (such as <code>height: 100%</code>), as resizing it alongside the root cancels the root's height transition.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <ToastVaryingHeightsDemo />
                </Example>
            </Examples>
        </DemoPage>
    );
}
